% ============= ABSTRACT =============
\begin{abstract}
In this work, a Generative Adversarial Fourier Neural Operator (GA-FNO) is presented for surrogate modeling of 2D Kolmogorov turbulence. The architecture employs two specialized discriminators: one evaluating the statistical coherence of generated trajectories and one evaluating Navier-Stokes residuals between frames. Zero-shot generalization to extended spatial domains is demonstrated through domain decomposition with cosine blending. Additionally, super-resolution capabilities and an Inverse Boundary Method for object simulation within generated flow fields are explored as post-processing extensions.

In the document Mathematical and Experimental details are discussed also autoregressive limitations are presented, in particular the emergence of spurious attractors in long-horizon rollouts, providing insights for future architecture and training improvements.

\keywords{Generative Adversarial Fourier Neural Operator \and GA-FNO \and turbulence surrogate modeling \and Fourier Neural Operator \and 2D Kolmogorov turbulence \and physics-informed discriminators \and Navier-Stokes residuals \and spectral convolution \and zero-shot super-resolution \and domain decomposition \and immersed boundary method}
\end{abstract}

% ============= INTRODUCTION =============
\section{Introduction}

The world of machine learning and numerical simulations are deeply tied, as machine learning methods can leverage simulation data to optimize and accelerate real life applications. Following this, we have approached the problem with a generative adversarial focus, looking for the probabilistic distribution that best replicates the non-linear dynamics of fluid flow.

Generative Adversarial Networks present a natural framework for this problem for three key reasons. First, they are the only generative framework that learns a distribution without explicit supervision — the generator does not need to know where the ground truth is, it only needs to fool the discriminator. Second, the adversarial nature is well suited for turbulence — two competing networks capture chaotic dynamics more naturally than deterministic models. Third, the discriminator acts as an implicit physical critic, learning what makes a trajectory physically plausible without being explicitly told.

Building on this, we extend the standard GAN framework with two specialized discriminators. The first evaluates the statistical coherence of the generated trajectories as a whole, while the second evaluates the Navier-Stokes residual between frames — ensuring that the generated flow respects the underlying physical equations at every timestep. The generator itself is implemented as a Neural Operator, which learns the solution operator of the flow dynamics directly in Fourier spectral space.

% ============= MATHEMATICAL FOUNDATIONS =============
\section{Mathematical Foundations}

\subsection{Navier-Stokes in Vorticity Form}

The 2D incompressible Navier-Stokes equations are expressed in
vorticity-stream function form. Let $\omega = \nabla \times u$
be the vorticity field. The governing equation is:

\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \omega}{\partial t} + u \frac{\partial \omega}{\partial x}
+ v \frac{\partial \omega}{\partial y} = \nu \nabla^2 \omega + f
\end{equation}

where $\nu = UL/Re$ is the kinematic viscosity, $f = \sin(Ky)$ is
the Kolmogorov forcing, and $(u, v)$ is the velocity field recovered
from the stream function $\psi$ via:

\begin{equation}
u = \frac{\partial \psi}{\partial y}, \quad
v = -\frac{\partial \psi}{\partial x}, \quad
\nabla^2 \psi = -\omega
\end{equation}

The Navier-Stokes residual, used as input to the physical
discriminator, is defined as:

\begin{equation}
R = \frac{\partial \omega}{\partial t} + u \frac{\partial \omega}{\partial x}
+ v \frac{\partial \omega}{\partial y} - \nu \nabla^2 \omega - f
\end{equation}

If $R = 0$ the field is physically consistent; otherwise $R \neq 0$
quantifies the violation. Discretizing in time:

\begin{equation}
\omega^{n+1} = \omega^n + \Delta t \left(
-\frac{\partial \psi}{\partial y}\frac{\partial \omega}{\partial x}
+ \frac{\partial \psi}{\partial x}\frac{\partial \omega}{\partial y}
+ \nu \nabla^2 \omega + f \right)
\end{equation}

\subsection{Spectral Solver}

The stream function is recovered spectrally in three steps.
First, the FFT maps the vorticity to Fourier space:

\begin{equation}
\hat{\omega}(k_x, k_y) = \sum_{x,y} \omega(x,y)\, e^{-i(k_x x + k_y y)}
\end{equation}

Second, the Poisson equation $\nabla^2 \psi = -\omega$ becomes
algebraic in Fourier space:

\begin{equation}
\hat{\psi}_{k_x, k_y} = \frac{\hat{\omega}_{k_x, k_y}}{k_x^2 + k_y^2}
\end{equation}

Third, the physical stream function is recovered via the
inverse FFT:

\begin{equation}
\psi(x,y) = \sum_{k_x, k_y} \hat{\psi}(k_x, k_y)\, e^{i(k_x x + k_y y)}
\end{equation}

from which the velocity components are obtained as
$u = \partial_y \psi$, $v = -\partial_x \psi$, computed
spectrally as $\hat{u} = ik_y \hat{\psi}$,
$\hat{v} = ik_x \hat{\psi}$, followed by IFFT.

\subsection{Spectral Convolution}

Given an input field $w(x,y)$, the spectral convolution layer first
maps it to Fourier space:

\begin{equation}
\hat{w}(k_x, k_y) = \mathcal{F}(w)
\end{equation}

A complex-valued linear transformation is then applied to the
spectral coefficients. The output representation $\hat{h}$ is
obtained by multiplying $\hat{w}$ with a trainable weight tensor:

\begin{equation}
\hat{h}_{o}(k_x,k_y) = \sum_{i=1}^{C_{\text{in}}}
W_{io}(k_x,k_y)\, \hat{w}_{i}(k_x,k_y)
\end{equation}

where $C_{\text{in}}$ and $C_{\text{out}}$ denote the number of
input and output channels, respectively, and
$W_{io}(k_x,k_y) \in \mathbb{C}$ are complex weights. These are
initialized as:

\begin{equation}
W = \frac{1}{C_{\text{in}} C_{\text{out}}}
\left( R_r + i R_i \right), \quad R_r, R_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)
\end{equation}

In practice, this operation is restricted to a truncated set of
Fourier modes, retaining only low-frequency components.

Finally, the output field in physical space is obtained via the
inverse transform:

\begin{equation}
h(x,y) = \mathcal{F}^{-1}(\hat{h})
\end{equation}

This formulation corresponds to a global convolution operator,
implemented efficiently through FFTs, where the kernel is learned
directly in spectral space.

\subsection{Wasserstein Distance and Gradient Penalty}

By the Kantorovich-Rubinstein duality, the Wasserstein-1 distance
between the real distribution $p_r$ and the generated distribution
$p_g$ is:

\begin{equation}
W_1(p_r, p_g) = \sup_{\|\nabla D\|_2 \leq 1}
\int D(x)\bigl(p_r(x) - p_g(x)\bigr)\, dx
\end{equation}

which in expectation form reads
$\mathbb{E}_{p_r}[D(x)] - \mathbb{E}_{p_g}[D(x)]$.
The 1-Lipschitz constraint $|D(x) - D(G(z))| \leq \|x - G(z)\|$
is enforced softly via a gradient penalty. The discriminator
loss is:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_D = \mathbb{E}[D(G(z))] - \mathbb{E}[D(x)]
+ \lambda\, \mathbb{E}_{\hat{x}}\!\left[\left(\|\nabla D(\hat{x})\|_2 - 1\right)^2\right]
\end{equation}

where the interpolated samples are drawn as:

\begin{equation}
\hat{x} = \alpha\, x + (1-\alpha)\, G(z), \quad \alpha \sim \mathcal{U}[0,1]
\end{equation}

\subsection{Inverse Boundary Method}

The Inverse Boundary Method is integrated into the vorticity–stream
function formulation of the 2D incompressible Navier–Stokes equations
to handle embedded obstacles within a periodic domain. The boundary
condition is enforced at the level of the Poisson solve through a
volume penalization approach. Given a predicted vorticity field
$\omega$, a free stream function $\psi$ is first obtained from the
Poisson equation:

\begin{equation}
\nabla^2 \psi - \chi(x,y)\,\frac{1}{\eta}\big(\psi - \psi_s\big) = -\omega
\end{equation}

where $\chi(x,y)$ is a smooth indicator function of the obstacle and
$\eta \ll 1$ is the penalization parameter. This enforces
$\psi \to \psi_b$ inside the obstacle, approximating the boundary
condition.

In practice, this is implemented iteratively. Starting from the free solution
$\psi^{(0)}$, the stream function is updated using a relaxed Jacobi-like iteration.
The residual at step $k$ is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\Delta \psi^{(k)} = -\nabla^2 \psi^{(k)} + \frac{\chi}{\eta}\bigl(\psi^{(k)} - \psi_s\bigr) - \omega
\end{equation}
and the update step is:
\begin{equation}
\psi^{(k+1)} = \psi^{(k)} + \beta\, \Delta \psi^{(k)}
\end{equation}
where $\beta \in (0,1]$ is a relaxation parameter. After convergence, the corrected stream function $\psi_{\text{corr}}$
is used to recover the velocity field and the vorticity:

\begin{equation}
\omega^{(k)} = -\nabla^2 \psi^{(k)}
\end{equation}

This modified projection step is embedded within each time update
of the Navier–Stokes dynamics, allowing the evolution of $\omega$
to respect obstacle-induced constraints without breaking the
spectral structure of the solver.

\subsection{Model Structure}

\subsubsection{Generator}

The generator $G_\theta$ is implemented as a Fourier Neural Operator
(FNO) that learns a mapping between consecutive vorticity fields.
Given the current state $\omega^n \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W}$ and
a latent vector $z \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)$, the input is first lifted
to a higher-dimensional feature space:

\begin{equation}
h_{ij}^{(0)} = W_{\text{lift}} \cdot [\delta{ij}^n,\, z]
\end{equation}

A sequence of $L$ FNO blocks is then applied. Each block consists
of a spectral convolution combined with a local linear transformation
and a nonlinearity:

\begin{equation}
h^{(\ell+1)} = \text{GELU}\!\left(
\frac{\mathcal{K}^{(\ell)}(h^{(\ell)}) + W_{\text{conv}}^{(\ell)}\, h^{(\ell)} - \mu^{(\ell)}}{\sigma^{(\ell)}}
\right), \quad \ell = 0, \dots, L-1
\end{equation}

where each block has independent trainable parameters, including
spectral weights, pointwise linear mappings, and normalization
statistics. This stacked structure enables the model to learn
progressively richer representations in Fourier space.

After the final block, the feature map
$h^{(L)} \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times H \times W}$ is projected back
to a scalar field:

\begin{equation}
h({\omega}) = W_{\text{proj}} \cdot h^{(L)}, \quad
h({\omega}) \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W}
\end{equation}

Finally, a residual connection produces the next state:

\begin{equation}
\omega^{n+1} = \omega^n + h({\omega})
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Discriminators}

Two discriminators with identical architectures are employed,
differing only in their input.

\paragraph{Statistical discriminator $D_{\text{stat}}$.}
Receives a full generated trajectory
\[
\mathcal{T} =
\begin{pmatrix}
\delta^0 \\
\delta^1 \\
\vdots \\
\delta^9
\end{pmatrix}
\in \mathbb{R}^{10 \times H \times W}
\]
A temporal convolutional encoder produces
$h \in \mathbb{R}^{64 \times H \times W}$:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T} \;\longrightarrow\; h \in \mathbb{R}^{64 \times H \times W}
\end{equation}

The convolution across channels is given by:

\begin{equation}
h_k(x,y) = \sum_{i=1}^{64} W_{k,i}\, \delta_i(x,y)
\end{equation}

\[
\begin{gathered}
h_1 = w_{1,1}\delta_1 + w_{1,2}\delta_2 + \cdots + w_{1,10}\delta_{10}
\\[10pt]
h_2 = w_{2,1}\delta_1 + w_{2,2}\delta_2 + \cdots + w_{2,10}\delta_{10}
\\[10pt]
\vdots
\\[10pt]
h_{64} = w_{64,1}\delta_1 + \cdots + w_{64,10}\delta_{10}
\end{gathered}
\]

The representation is then processed by FNO blocks, preserving
the dimensionality:

\begin{equation}
h \;\longrightarrow\; h' \in \mathbb{R}^{64 \times H \times W}
\end{equation}

A spatial average is applied:

\begin{equation}
\bar{h}_k = \frac{1}{HW} \sum_{x,y} h'_k(x,y)
\end{equation}

yielding $\bar{h} \in \mathbb{R}^{64}$, which is passed through
two linear layers with GELU activation:

\begin{equation}
D_{\text{stat}}(\mathcal{T}) = L_{n_1} \cdot
\text{GELU}(L_{n_2} \cdot \bar{h})
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Physical discriminator $D_{\text{phys}}$.}
Receives the sequence of Navier-Stokes residuals
\[
R =
\begin{pmatrix}
R^0 \\
R^1 \\
\vdots \\
R^{T-2}
\end{pmatrix}
\in \mathbb{R}^{(T-1) \times H \times W}
\]
A temporal convolutional encoder produces
$h \in \mathbb{R}^{64 \times H \times W}$:

\begin{equation}
R \;\longrightarrow\; h \in \mathbb{R}^{64 \times H \times W}
\end{equation}

The convolution across channels is given by:

\begin{equation}
h_k(x,y) = \sum_{i=1}^{64} W_{k,i}\, R_i(x,y)
\end{equation}

The representation is then processed by FNO blocks, preserving
the dimensionality:

\begin{equation}
h \;\longrightarrow\; h' \in \mathbb{R}^{64 \times H \times W}
\end{equation}

A spatial average is applied:

\begin{equation}
\bar{h}_k = \frac{1}{HW} \sum_{x,y} h'_k(x,y)
\end{equation}

yielding $\bar{h} \in \mathbb{R}^{64}$, which is passed through
two linear layers with GELU activation:

\begin{equation}
D_{\text{phys}}(R) = L_{n_1} \cdot
\text{GELU}(L_{n_2} \cdot \bar{h})
\end{equation}

\subsection{Objective Function}

The full training objective couples both discriminators.
Each discriminator is updated independently with its own
gradient penalty:

\begin{align}
\mathcal{L}_{D_{\text{stat}}} &= \mathbb{E}[D_{\text{stat}}(T_{\text{fake}})]
- \mathbb{E}[D_{\text{stat}}(T_{\text{real}})] + \lambda\, GP_{\text{stat}} \\
\mathcal{L}_{D_{\text{phys}}} &= \mathbb{E}[D_{\text{phys}}(R_{\text{fake}})]
- \mathbb{E}[D_{\text{phys}}(R_{\text{real}})] + \lambda\, GP_{\text{phys}}
\end{align}

The Wasserstein distance estimates used to monitor training are:

\begin{align}
W_{\text{stat}} &\approx \mathbb{E}[D_{\text{stat}}(\omega_{\text{real}})]
- \mathbb{E}[D_{\text{stat}}(\omega_{\text{fake}})] \\
W_{\text{phys}} &\approx \mathbb{E}[D_{\text{phys}}(R_{\text{real}})]
- \mathbb{E}[D_{\text{phys}}(R_{\text{fake}})]
\end{align}

The generator is trained to minimize:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_G = -\mathbb{E}[D_{\text{stat}}(\omega_{\text{fake}})]
              - \mathbb{E}[D_{\text{phys}}(R_{\text{fake}})]
\end{equation}

with gradients computed via the chain rule through both
discriminator paths:

\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}_G}{\partial \theta_G} =
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \omega_{\text{fake}}}
\cdot \frac{\partial \omega_{\text{fake}}}{\partial \theta_G}
+ \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial R_{\text{fake}}}
\cdot \frac{\partial R_{\text{fake}}}{\partial \omega_{\text{fake}}}
\cdot \frac{\partial \omega_{\text{fake}}}{\partial \theta_G}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Autoregressive Rollout}

The generator produces trajectories autoregressively.
Given an initial condition $\omega^0$, each subsequent
frame is obtained by feeding the previous prediction
back into the generator:

\begin{equation}
\omega^{t+1} = G(\omega^t, z), \quad z \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)
\end{equation}

A full trajectory of length $T$ is therefore:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T} = \{\omega^0,\, G(\omega^0, z),\,
G^2(\omega^0, z),\, \ldots,\, G^T(\omega^0, z)\}
\end{equation}

This is equivalent to approximating the continuous-time
operator $\partial_t \omega = F(\omega)$ by a discrete
map $\omega^{t+1} \approx \omega^t + h(\omega^t)$,
where $h$ is learned by the FNO. The stochastic latent
vector $z$ is resampled at each step, allowing the model
to represent the distributional uncertainty of turbulent
trajectories rather than collapsing to a single deterministic path.

% ============= METHODOLOGY =============
\section{Methodology}

\subsection{Dataset}

The dataset is sourced from the \texttt{scaomath/navier-stokes-dataset} repository
on Hugging Face \cite{scaomath2024}. It consists of isotropic turbulence trajectories
modeled by the 2D incompressible Navier-Stokes equations, generated using a
pseudo-spectral solver with a 4th-order Runge-Kutta scheme for the convection term.
Different initial conditions with varying peak wavenumbers of $\mathcal{O}(1)$ are
included, all converging to the Kolmogorov inverse energy cascade. The dataset
contains 4 different fields including vorticity, differential, stream and residue including 1,152 trajectories of 100 timesteps each on a $64 \times 64$ spatial grid each,
yielding a tensor of shape $(4, 1152, 100, 64, 64)$ for the implementation we only used the vorticty field represented by
$\omega(x, t) = \nabla \times u$.

\subsection{Preprocessing}
The vorticity field is first normalized using a min–max scaling to the range [0,1]. It is then standardized by subtracting the global mean and dividing by the global standard deviation computed over the entire dataset. Sliding windows of length $T_{seq} = 10$ are constructed over each trajectory, producing input–output pairs $(\omega_n, \omega_{n+1})$ for autoregressive training. This results in a total of 103,680 training windows. An 80/20 train–validation split is applied at the window level.

\subsection{Implementation}

The model is implemented in PyTorch and trained on a CUDA-enabled GPU.
The architecture follows a dual-discriminator Wasserstein GAN with Gradient
Penalty (WGAN-GP) framework, where the generator is a Fourier Neural Operator.

The generator $G_\theta$ receives the current vorticity field $\omega_n$
alongside a stochastic latent vector $z \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)$ and produces
the next timestep $\omega_{n+1}$. Trajectories are generated autoregressively,
feeding each predicted frame as input to the next step.

Two specialized discriminators are maintained independently throughout training,
each with its own optimizer, learning rate scheduler, and Wasserstein distance
estimate. The statistical discriminator $D_{stat}$ receives complete generated
trajectories and judges their distributional realism. The physical discriminator
$D_{phys}$ receives the Navier-Stokes residual field computed between consecutive
frames and judges physical consistency independently of statistical appearance.

The Navier-Stokes residual is computed spectrally at each training step, with
viscosity $\nu$ and forcing $f$ sampled stochastically to reflect the variability
observed in the dataset. Both discriminators are updated $n_{critic} = 2$ times
per generator step. Gradient penalty is applied independently to each discriminator
over interpolated inputs in their respective input spaces.

Training progress is monitored through five validation metrics computed at the
end of each epoch over the held-out validation set: mean squared error against
ground truth, Navier-Stokes residual magnitude, kinetic energy, enstrophy, and
spectral correlation. Two independent Wasserstein distance estimates — one per
discriminator — serve as the primary indicators of adversarial equilibrium.

