% ============= RESULTS =============
\section{Results}

\subsection{Training Metrics}
Both discriminators were trained with a learning
rate of $10^{-4}$, while the generator learning rate was halved to $5 \times 10^{-5}$
after epoch 9 via a \texttt{ReduceLROnPlateau} scheduler conditioned on validation MSE.

\subsubsection{Wasserstein Distances and Adversarial Losses}

The Wasserstein distances for both critics increased monotonically during the first
ten epochs and subsequently plateaued, indicating that the discriminators reached a
stable equilibrium with the generator. Specifically, $W_\text{stat}$ stabilized
around $111$ and $W_\text{phys}$ around $1{,}320$. This is consistent with standard
WGAN dynamics: the critic score reflects distributional distance rather than
classification confidence, and a plateau indicates that the generator produces
samples whose distribution is consistently separated from the real data manifold
by a stable margin.

The generator loss $\mathcal{L}_G$ grew steadily throughout training, reaching
approximately $2{,}377$ by epoch 30. This is the expected behavior in WGAN-GP
training: as the discriminators improve their ability to distinguish real from
generated samples, the adversarial signal pushes the generator loss upward. The
discriminator losses $\mathcal{L}_{D_\text{stat}}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{D_\text{phys}}$
remained negative throughout training, reflecting the Wasserstein objective in which
critics are rewarded for assigning higher scores to real samples and lower scores
to generated ones.

