\subsubsection{Spectral Diagnostics at Epoch 30}

The spectral diagnostics at epoch 30 evaluate the quality of the generated turbulent
fields in Fourier space, assessing whether the generator reproduces the correct
distribution of energy across scales, the correct inter-scale energy transfer, and
phase coherence with the ground-truth fields.

\paragraph{Enstrophy spectrum.}
The enstrophy spectrum $Z(k)$ of the generated fields closely follows the real
spectrum across the resolved wavenumber range $k \in [1, 40]$. Both spectra peak
near $k \approx 3$--$4$ and exhibit a plateau at intermediate wavenumbers consistent
with the theoretical $k^{-1}$ scaling for 2D turbulence in the enstrophy cascade
regime. At high wavenumbers ($k > 10$), the generated spectrum exhibits slightly more
variability but remains within one order of magnitude of the real spectrum.

\paragraph{Palinstrophy spectrum.}
The palinstrophy spectrum $P(k)$, which measures the squared gradient of vorticity
and is sensitive to small-scale structures, is well reproduced by the generator across
the full wavenumber range. Both spectra increase monotonically with $k$ and lie well
above the $k^{+1}$ reference scaling, indicating that palinstrophy is concentrated at
small scales as expected in 2D turbulence. The close agreement suggests that the
generator captures fine-scale vorticity gradients accurately, despite these not being
explicitly penalized during training.

\paragraph{Enstrophy transfer spectrum.}
The enstrophy transfer spectrum $T(k)$ characterizes the nonlinear inter-scale
enstrophy flux. Both the real and generated spectra exhibit the same qualitative
structure: positive transfer at large scales ($k < 4$), a pronounced negative peak
near $k \approx 4$--$5$, and near-zero transfer at small scales. The generator
reproduces the location and approximate depth of the negative peak, though it slightly
overestimates the magnitude. This confirms that the adversarial training promotes
correct nonlinear cascade dynamics without explicit transfer spectrum supervision.

\paragraph{Spectral correlation.}
The spectral correlation $C(k)$ measures phase coherence between generated and real
fields in Fourier space, with $C(k) = 1$ indicating perfect agreement and $C(k) = 0$
indicating no coherence. At large scales ($k < 4$), the correlation is near or above
$1.0$, indicating that the generator faithfully reproduces dominant large-scale
structures. The correlation decreases progressively at intermediate and small scales,
dropping below $0.5$ near $k \approx 8$ and approaching zero for $k > 15$. This
decay is expected for an autoregressive model without explicit phase supervision.
The final mean spectral correlation of approximately $0.30$ across all wavenumbers
indicates meaningful but limited phase coherence, consistent with the model learning
the correct distribution of turbulent structures rather than predicting individual
deterministic realizations.

