\subsection{Inverse Boundary Method}
The IBM zero-shot experiment evaluates the ability of the pretrained FNO generator
to simulate vorticity dynamics in the presence of solid obstacles without any
fine-tuning. Three obstacle geometries were tested: a centered circle of radius
$r = 0.08$, an off-center circle of radius $r = 0.12$, and a centered rectangle.
In each case, the immersed boundary condition $\psi = 0$ inside the obstacle was
enforced at every autoregressive step via iterative volume penalization applied
to the stream function recovered from the predicted vorticity field through a
spectral Poisson solver. The algorithmic details of the immersed boundary
implementation are provided in the Mathematical Foundations section.

The enstrophy evolution reveals that the penalization
significantly alters the vorticity field produced by the surrogate model.
This is primarily due to the reflection and scattering of the stream function
induced by the obstacle, which modifies the local vorticity gradients and
generates secondary flow structures.

