\subsection{FID Distance}

\subsubsection{Baseline Comparison}

The quality of the generated trajectories was assessed using the Fréchet Distance
(FD) computed in the feature space of the two trained discriminators, following the
methodology of the Fréchet Inception Distance but substituting the Inception network
with domain-specific critics tailored to turbulent vorticity fields. Features from
\texttt{D\_stat} and \texttt{D\_phys} were concatenated to form a joint
representation capturing both statistical and physical characteristics of each
trajectory.

Formally, let $\boldsymbol{\mu}_r, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_r$ and
$\boldsymbol{\mu}_g, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_g$ denote the mean vectors and covariance
matrices of the concatenated features computed from real and generated trajectories,
respectively. The Fréchet Distance is then defined as:
\begin{equation}
\text{FD} = \left\| \boldsymbol{\mu}_r - \boldsymbol{\mu}_g \right\|_2^2
+ \operatorname{Tr}\left( \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_r + \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_g
- 2\left( \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_r \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_g \right)^{1/2} \right)
\end{equation}
The FD was computed over $10$ independent random subsets of $100$ validation
samples and reported as mean $\pm$ standard deviation.

As shown in Table~\ref{tab:fd}, the reference value $\text{FD}(\text{GT}, \text{GT}) = 0.0023 \pm 0.0005$
establishes the natural variability floor of the metric under sampling noise.
The GAN-based generator (Gen) achieves $\text{FD} = 0.2507 \pm 0.0159$, while
{\color{red} the MSE-trained baseline obtains $\text{FD} = 0.0783 \pm 0.0159$. The lower FD
of the baseline is explained by its training objective: direct MSE supervision
with a larger model capacity ($\texttt{hidden\_ch} = 64$ versus $32$ for Gen)
encourages the generator to produce trajectories that are metrically close to the
ground truth in an $\ell_2$ sense, which is directly reflected in the discriminator
feature space. The GAN generator, by contrast, optimizes a distributional objective
and does not minimize pointwise reconstruction error, leading to a higher FD despite
producing trajectories with richer turbulent structure and better spectral
characteristics.

Visual inspection of the feature embeddings reveals that
the Baseline cluster overlaps substantially with the Ground Truth distribution,
whereas the Gen cluster is shifted, indicating a systematic distributional offset.
Notably, this offset is not fixed: with extended training, the generator's feature
distribution gradually drifts toward the real manifold, suggesting that the
reported FD for the GAN model could be further reduced by additional epochs or
improved hyperparameter tuning.}

