\subsubsection{Fourier Spurious Attractor}

A notable failure mode was identified during autoregressive rollouts
extended beyond the training horizon. When the generator is unrolled
for more than approximately $75$ steps — well past the training sequence
length of $10$ — the predicted vorticity fields begin to exhibit a
spurious diagonal wavenumber pattern accompanied by vertical striping
artifacts (Fig.~\ref{fig:attractor}).

This behavior is consistent with the emergence of a non-physical
attractor in Fourier space. In the autoregressive setting, each
prediction is fed back as input, causing the trajectory to explore
regions of the latent space and energy spectrum that lie far from the
training distribution. Under these conditions, the Fourier Neural
Operator tends to revert to the dominant linear dynamics embedded in
its learned weights — primarily those associated with horizontal
wavenumber bands. The observed spurious diagonal and striping patterns
correspond to residual Fourier components that were present during
training but become unstable and self-amplifying when iterated beyond
the intended temporal window. This highlights a known vulnerability of
autoregressive FNO rollouts: the accumulation of spectral bias errors
leads to a gradual drift away from the physical manifold of the
Navier–Stokes equations.

